From: jmgasper <jmgasper@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 22:53:47 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 09/14] zink/hgl: two defects a soak would only have found by
 luck

The application's thread replaces the screen's frame buffer on a mode
set while the window's thread may be reading it to put the last frame
back, which is a use after free waiting for someone to change their
resolution at the wrong moment. The replacement now happens with the
lock held; finding it unchanged, which is every frame but the first and
the ones after a mode set, still takes no lock.

The frame is copied into the screen with no conversion, but nothing
checked that the two agree on what a pixel is.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_screen.c        | 26 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../targets/haiku-zink/ZinkRenderer.cpp       |  5 ++++
 2 files changed, 31 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_screen.c b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_screen.c
index ad63d04..01085cb 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_screen.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_screen.c
@@ -1498,15 +1498,41 @@ present_into_host_memory(struct zink_screen *screen, struct zink_context *ctx,
 
 
 #ifdef __HAIKU__
+static bool acquire_scanout_locked(struct zink_screen *screen,
+                                   const struct hgl_scanout_target *target);
+
 // Take hold of the screen's frame buffer, which the graphics driver publishes
 // and shares. It is video memory, so a frame copied there does not cross the
 // bus at all - a 1920x1080 frame costs about a millisecond instead of three.
+//
+// This runs on the application's thread while the window's thread may be
+// putting the last frame back through the same buffer, so the buffer is only
+// ever replaced with the lock held. Finding it unchanged, which is every frame
+// but the first and the ones after a mode set, takes no lock at all.
 static bool
 acquire_scanout(struct zink_screen *screen,
                 const struct hgl_scanout_target *target)
 {
    const uint32_t bytes_per_row = target->bytes_per_row;
 
+   /* The common case: the buffer we hold is still the one on screen. */
+   if (screen->scanout.buffer != VK_NULL_HANDLE &&
+       screen->scanout.bits == target->bits &&
+       screen->scanout.row_pitch == bytes_per_row)
+      return true;
+
+   simple_mtx_lock(&screen->scanout.represent_lock);
+   const bool ok = acquire_scanout_locked(screen, target);
+   simple_mtx_unlock(&screen->scanout.represent_lock);
+   return ok;
+}
+
+static bool
+acquire_scanout_locked(struct zink_screen *screen,
+                       const struct hgl_scanout_target *target)
+{
+   const uint32_t bytes_per_row = target->bytes_per_row;
+
    if (screen->scanout.buffer != VK_NULL_HANDLE) {
       if (screen->scanout.bits == target->bits &&
           screen->scanout.row_pitch == bytes_per_row)
diff --git a/src/gallium/targets/haiku-zink/ZinkRenderer.cpp b/src/gallium/targets/haiku-zink/ZinkRenderer.cpp
index 4e00fe5..1c6631d 100644
--- a/src/gallium/targets/haiku-zink/ZinkRenderer.cpp
+++ b/src/gallium/targets/haiku-zink/ZinkRenderer.cpp
@@ -234,6 +234,11 @@ ZinkRenderer::ScanoutTarget(struct hgl_scanout_target *target)
 		return false;
 	}
 
+	// The frame is copied into the screen a pixel at a time with no conversion,
+	// so the two have to agree on what a pixel is.
+	if (fInfo->pixel_format != B_RGB32 && fInfo->pixel_format != B_RGBA32)
+		return false;
+
 	target->origin_x = fInfo->window_bounds.left;
 	target->origin_y = fInfo->window_bounds.top;
 	target->bits = fInfo->bits;
